Architecture
------------

### Page Format

RECNO pages use a custom page format optimized for in-place updates:

```
Page Layout:
+----------------+
| Page Header    |  (32 bytes)
+----------------+
| Item Pointers  |
+----------------+
| ...Free Space..|
+----------------+
| Tuple Data     |
+----------------+
```

### Tuple Format

RECNO tuples include timestamp information for MVCC:

```
Tuple Header:
- Length (4 bytes)
- Number of attributes (2 bytes)
- Flags (2 bytes)
- Commit timestamp (8 bytes)
- Transaction timestamp (8 bytes)
- Current TID (6 bytes)
- Command ID (4 bytes)
- Info mask (2 bytes)
- Null bitmap + compression info
```

### MVCC Implementation

RECNO uses timestamp-based visibility checking:

```c
bool RecnoTupleVisible(RecnoTupleHeader *tuple, uint64 snapshot_ts, uint64 xact_ts)
{
    if (snapshot_ts == 0)
	return true;  // SnapshotAny

    if (tuple->t_flags & RECNO_TUPLE_DELETED)
	return tuple->t_commit_ts > snapshot_ts;

    return tuple->t_commit_ts <= snapshot_ts;
}
```

Overflow Pages
--------------

RECNO implements integrated overflow page storage for large attributes,
replacing PostgreSQL's traditional TOAST (The Oversized-Attribute Storage
Technique) mechanism with a Derby-inspired design that keeps overflow data
within the same relation.


Why Overflow Pages?
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

When a tuple contains attributes larger than a threshold (default 8KB), storing
the entire tuple inline would be wasteful and could prevent efficient page
utilization. Overflow pages solve this by:

1. Storing large attribute values in separate overflow pages
2. Keeping a short inline prefix in the main tuple for common access patterns
3. Maintaining overflow data in the same relation (unlike TOAST's separate table)
4. Using spatial locality optimization to reduce I/O during overflow fetches


Architecture
~~~~~~~~~~~~

RECNO overflow pages use a Derby-inspired spatial locality optimization where
multiple overflow records from different tuples can be stored on the same
overflow page. This differs from TOAST's approach of one-toast-tuple-per-chunk
and provides better I/O efficiency.

Overflow Record Format:

    +---------------------------+
    | RecnoOverflowRecordHeader |  (fixed size)
    +---------------------------+
    | Overflow Data             |  (variable size)
    +---------------------------+

The RecnoOverflowRecordHeader contains:
- Length of this overflow record
- Block number of next overflow record in chain (or InvalidBlockNumber)
- Item number of next overflow record
- Checksum for integrity verification

When an attribute exceeds the threshold:

1. The attribute is split into one or more overflow records
2. Each overflow record is stored on an overflow page
3. The main tuple stores an RecnoOverflowPointer (block number + item offset)
4. Overflow records are chained together for attributes larger than one record
5. Multiple overflow records may be placed on the same page (spatial locality)


Overflow Chain Example:

    Main Tuple Page:
    +----------------------+
    | Tuple Header         |
    | ...                  |
    | large_col: [OVF PTR]-+----+
    | ...                  |    |
    +----------------------+    |
                                |
                                v
    Overflow Page 100:          |
    +----------------------+    |
    | Record 1 (Item 1) <-------+
    |   Data: [10KB]     |
    |   Next: 100/2   ----+
    +----------------------+|
    | Record 2 (Item 2) <--+
    |   Data: [8KB]      |
    |   Next: 101/1   ----+
    +----------------------+|
                            |
    Overflow Page 101:      |
    +----------------------+|
    | Record 1 (Item 1) <--+
    |   Data: [5KB]      |
    |   Next: INVALID    |
    +----------------------+

In this example, a 23KB attribute is stored as three overflow records:
- Two records on page 100 (items 1 and 2)
- One record on page 101 (item 1)

Notice that records from the same chain can reside on the same page (100/1 and
100/2). This spatial locality optimization reduces I/O when fetching the entire
chain, but requires careful buffer management to avoid locking the same buffer
multiple times during traversal.


Comparison with TOAST
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

| Aspect | TOAST (Heap) | RECNO Overflow | Notes |
|--------|--------------|----------------|-------|
| Storage location | Separate pg_toast table | Same relation | RECNO simpler structure |
| Threshold | ~2KB (TOAST_TUPLE_THRESHOLD) | Configurable (default 8KB) | RECNO more aggressive |
| Inline prefix | Yes (up to TOAST_MAX_CHUNK_SIZE) | Yes (configurable) | Both support prefix |
| Spatial locality | No (one chunk per row) | Yes (multiple records per page) | RECNO optimization |
| Compression | Yes (pglz) | Yes (DELTA, DICT, LZ4, ZSTD) | RECNO more algorithms |
| External storage | Yes (separate file) | No (inline in relation) | RECNO keeps data together |
| Catalog overhead | Requires toast table catalog entry | No separate catalog entry | RECNO simpler |


Integration with RECNO Operations
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

INSERT:
When inserting a tuple with large attributes, RecnoFormTupleWithOverflow()
is called to:
1. Identify attributes exceeding the overflow threshold
2. Allocate overflow pages using the FSM
3. Create overflow record chains
4. Store overflow pointers in the main tuple
5. WAL-log overflow record creation

FETCH (SELECT):
When fetching a tuple with overflow attributes, RecnoFetchOverflowColumn()
is called to:
1. Read the overflow pointer from the main tuple
2. Follow the overflow chain, reading each overflow record
3. Reassemble the complete attribute value
4. Return the materialized value to the executor

IMPORTANT: The fetch path must handle the case where multiple overflow records
reside on the same page. This requires:
- Checking if the next record is on an already-locked buffer
- Reusing the buffer lock instead of attempting to lock again
- Properly managing buffer pins across the chain traversal

UPDATE:
When updating a tuple with overflow attributes:
1. If the old value had overflow data, clean up old overflow records
2. If the new value requires overflow, create new overflow chains
3. Reuse overflow pages when possible (if size unchanged or smaller)
4. Update overflow pointers in the main tuple

DELETE:
When deleting a tuple with overflow attributes:
1. Mark overflow records as deleted
2. Overflow space reclamation is deferred to VACUUM
3. WAL-log overflow record deletion for crash recovery

VACUUM:
RecnoVacuumOverflowRecords() reclaims space from deleted overflow records:
1. Scan pages looking for overflow records
2. Check visibility of the owning tuples
3. Reclaim space from orphaned overflow records
4. Update FSM to reflect freed space
5. Handle same-page buffer transitions carefully (spatial locality)


Buffer Management Considerations
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The spatial locality optimization (multiple overflow records per page) creates
a buffer management challenge: when following an overflow chain, the next
record in the chain may reside on the same page as the current record. This
means the buffer is already locked, and attempting to lock it again would
cause a "buffer already locked" assertion failure.

The current implementation addresses this by:

1. In the main tuple fetch path (recno_index_fetch_tuple), unlocking the main
   tuple's buffer before calling RecnoTupleToSlotWithOverflow(). This prevents
   deadlock if overflow data resides on the same page as the main tuple.

2. In RecnoFetchOverflowColumn(), detecting same-page transitions and reusing
   the buffer instead of attempting to acquire a new lock.

3. In VACUUM's RecnoVacuumOverflowRecords(), explicitly checking if the next
   overflow record is on the current page and reusing the buffer lock.

This is similar to how heap's nodeModifyTable.c handles UPDATE operations,
where the executor creates the new tuple by reading from a buffer, then calls
table_tuple_update() which may need to lock the same buffer again.


Configuration
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Overflow-related GUC parameters:

- recno_overflow_threshold (integer, default 8192)
  Attributes larger than this threshold (in bytes) are stored in overflow pages.

- recno_overflow_inline_prefix (integer, default 128)
  Number of bytes to keep inline in the main tuple as a prefix. Useful for
  queries that only need the beginning of large attributes (e.g., LIKE 'prefix%').

- recno_enable_overflow (boolean, default on)
  Master switch to enable/disable overflow page usage.


Performance Characteristics
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Advantages:
- Better I/O locality than TOAST (overflow data in same relation)
- Spatial locality optimization reduces seeks during chain traversal
- No catalog overhead for toast tables
- Inline prefix enables optimization of prefix-matching queries
- Integrated with FSM for efficient space management

Trade-offs:
- Slightly higher complexity in buffer management (same-page transitions)
- Cannot share overflow data across relations (TOAST can with shared toast tables)
- Main relation grows larger (includes overflow pages)


Monitoring
~~~~~~~~~~

Query overflow page statistics:

    SELECT * FROM recno_overflow_stats('table_name');

Returns:
- Number of overflow pages
- Total overflow data size
- Average chain length
- Number of orphaned overflow records (need VACUUM)


Known Limitations
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

1. No deduplication: Identical large values are stored separately (TOAST
   has similar limitation)

2. No external storage: All overflow data is in-relation (TOAST supports
   external BLOB storage)

3. Single-relation only: Cannot share overflow pages across relations

4. Chain length: Very large attributes (>100MB) may have long chains with
   many overflow records, impacting fetch performance


Future Enhancements
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Potential improvements for overflow page handling:

1. Adaptive threshold: Automatically adjust overflow threshold based on
   workload patterns

2. Compression integration: Compress overflow data before storing (LZ4/ZSTD)

3. Parallel fetch: Fetch multiple overflow chains in parallel for wide tuples

4. Smart prefetching: Prefetch overflow pages when main tuple is accessed

5. Deduplication: Hash-based deduplication of large attribute values


WAL Logging
~~~~~~~~~~~

Overflow operations are fully WAL-logged for crash recovery:

- XLOG_RECNO_OVERFLOW_INSERT: Log overflow record creation
- XLOG_RECNO_OVERFLOW_DELETE: Log overflow record deletion
- XLOG_RECNO_OVERFLOW_VACUUM: Log bulk overflow cleanup during VACUUM

Each WAL record contains enough information to REDO the operation on a standby
or during crash recovery, including block numbers, item numbers, and data
checksums.


