Feature Parity with Heap Storage
---------------------------------

This section compares RECNO with PostgreSQL's default heap storage to help
understand architectural differences and feature compatibility.

### Core Storage and MVCC

| Feature | Heap | RECNO | Status | Notes |
|---------|------|-------|--------|-------|
| **Update mechanism** | Append-only (new tuple version) | In-place update when possible | Working | RECNO's key advantage; falls back to new-page when tuple grows |
| **MVCC visibility** | XID-based (xmin/xmax) | Timestamp-based (commit_ts) | Working | Semantically equivalent |
| **Snapshot isolation** | Yes (XID snapshots) | Yes (timestamp snapshots) | Working | All isolation levels supported |
| **Serializable isolation** | Yes (SSI with SIREAD locks) | Yes (SSI with anti-deps) | Partial | Simplified pivot detection only; full cycle detection not implemented |
| **Transaction ID wraparound** | Yes (requires periodic VACUUM) | No (64-bit timestamps) | Working | RECNO eliminates wraparound |
| **Tuple versioning** | HOT for same-page updates | N/A (already in-place) | N/A | RECNO doesn't need HOT |
| **Dead tuple cleanup** | Reactive (VACUUM) | Proactive (defrag + VACUUM) | Partial | VACUUM stub exists but does not fully reclaim dead tuple space |
| **Page pruning** | Opportunistic (hint bits) | Proactive (defrag during ops) | Working | Opportunistic defrag implemented |
| **Tuple header size** | 23-27 bytes | 36 bytes | N/A | RECNO 9-13 bytes larger per tuple |
| **XID fields (xmin/xmax)** | Used for MVCC visibility | Used only for locking | Working | Different usage in RECNO |
| **Prepared transactions** | Yes | Yes (timestamp-based) | Working | Uses command ID tracking |
| **Savepoints** | Yes | Yes (via command ID) | Working | Savepoints supported |

